WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:11.100
- Hi, everyone, Sal
here from Khan Academy.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.120
Welcome to our daily homeroom livestream.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:16.312
For those of you who
don't know what this is

00:00:16.312 --> 00:00:18.380
(laughs) or what Khan Academy is,

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:19.880
Khan Academy is a not-for-profit

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:21.230
with a mission of providing a free,

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:23.410
world-class education
for anyone, anywhere.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:26.840
And over the last many years,
we've been building resources

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:28.970
from early learning all
the way through elementary,

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:31.238
middle, high school, early
college, math, science,

00:00:31.238 --> 00:00:34.030
English language arts,
and we always imagine that

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:35.510
it would be used in conjunction

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:37.890
with normal, physical classrooms

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.420
or it could be used by
students outside of classrooms.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:42.090
Maybe students in parts of the world

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:44.580
who might not have access to materials

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.350
or certain types of courses.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:48.060
So we could've never
foreseen the situation

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:49.800
that the world is now in,

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.920
but as soon as we saw the
school closures happen

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:53.040
many weeks ago now,

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.440
it seems like a lifetime
ago, I know, for many of us,

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.250
we realized that it's our
duty as a not-for-profit

00:00:58.250 --> 00:00:59.430
with this mission to step up.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:00.840
And so, you can see here the resources

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.180
that we've been putting together

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:04.470
to essentially keep everyone learning.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.440
And so, we've been doing
things like this livestream,

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.370
which is just a way for
us to stay connected

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:10.931
and answer any questions you might have.

00:01:10.931 --> 00:01:14.017
But also, we've been
doing parent webinars,

00:01:14.017 --> 00:01:16.200
teacher webinars, we've
been putting together

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.270
daily schedules for how you
could structure your day

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:20.870
or your children's day
or your student's day

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:21.920
if you're a teacher.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:25.130
We just put out learning plans
so that folks can understand

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:27.050
how they can pace themselves
over the course of

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:29.690
the next six weeks to 20 weeks.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:32.023
We ideally think most people
should leverage the summer

00:01:32.023 --> 00:01:34.930
so that you don't have that learning loss

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:36.660
and you're able to go to next school year

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:39.020
with as much learning under
your belt as possible,

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:40.415
as prepared as possible.

00:01:40.415 --> 00:01:41.680
So, that's the goal of this.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:43.230
We bring on guests every now and then.

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:44.670
I do wanna give a special shout-out.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:45.690
We are a not-for-profit.

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:48.100
We are funded through
philanthropic donations.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.840
We were already running
at a deficit before this

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:52.640
and our deficit has increased

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.890
because now our load on
our servers, et cetera,

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:58.229
are up over two and a half
times what we normally see.

00:01:58.229 --> 00:02:01.540
I wanna give a special
thanks to Bank of America,

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:04.930
AT&amp;T, Fastly, Google.org, and Novartis,

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:06.500
who are companies that have stepped up

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.598
just in the last few
weeks in order to really

00:02:08.598 --> 00:02:12.610
support our response to what's going on

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:14.420
with the school closures, and of course,

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:17.260
our long-time donors who
help Khan Academy exist,

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:18.850
but we do need more support.

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:20.930
Whether it's an individual
donor who's able to give

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:23.630
five, $10, all the way
up to a corporation,

00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:26.460
everything helps and allows
us to support more folks,

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:28.696
especially folks who might not
otherwise be able to afford

00:02:28.696 --> 00:02:31.750
or have access to resources like that.

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:34.250
So, today, we're going to just
do another ask me anything.

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:36.244
We have some exciting guests tomorrow.

00:02:36.244 --> 00:02:39.160
We have a vice president from
the college board to answer.

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:41.147
I know there's a lot of
questions around the SAT

00:02:41.147 --> 00:02:43.960
and implications for when
the testing dates are

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:45.790
and implications for college admissions,

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.850
so we're gonna have a
very robust conversation

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:49.030
about that tomorrow.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:51.940
But for today, it'll
be more of me and Dan,

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:53.701
my colleague is gonna help support

00:02:53.701 --> 00:02:55.620
just any questions y'all have.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.810
And I know there was a ton
of questions from yesterday.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.560
We can answer some of those,

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:04.560
but happy to take any
questions y'all have.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:09.713
So let's see, I have this
one question I'm seeing is,

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.950
so from YouTube, Nikhil Govendar says,

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:14.430
Hi, Sal, I'm currently
working on a project

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:16.650
that requires you to build an app.

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:19.870
Could you please give me
any suggestions or ideas

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:20.730
on building an app?

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:21.882
Well, this is interesting, and actually,

00:03:21.882 --> 00:03:24.420
if anyone else has suggestions or ideas

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:26.287
for Nikhil's app, feel free (laughs).

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:29.790
And Nikhil, whoever's
suggestion you take, you know,

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:33.210
you owe them a cut, and
if you leverage ours,

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:35.660
donate to Khan Academy. (laughs)

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:38.330
So, app ideas.

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:40.990
You know, it's funny,
I'm always intrigued.

00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:43.090
People before, on previous livestreams,

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:44.810
have said, Sal, if you weren't
working on Khan Academy,

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:46.690
what other things would you wanna work on?

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:48.370
And I'm fascinated by anything

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:49.940
that can help make other people,

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:52.730
help people have richer lives

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:55.240
or maybe explore that
whole side of happiness

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:57.131
and I had thought about it.

00:03:57.131 --> 00:04:00.400
As far as I'm aware, no one's
building it, maybe someone is,

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.810
a app that helps build relationships.

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:04.480
It could be relationships with friends,

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:06.150
it could be relationships with co-workers,

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:08.170
it could be relationships with a partner,

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:12.570
where you're able to
essentially rate how you feel

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:13.940
about that relationship

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:16.200
and then you can benchmark
your relationship

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:17.800
relative to other relationships

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:18.633
'cause you would have the data

00:04:18.633 --> 00:04:21.010
on how other people are
feeling about the relationship.

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:22.590
So I think that could
be an interesting app

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:24.380
if you're in a corporation or at a school

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:26.730
to measure kind of team
health or sentiment.

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:29.108
You could imagine in kind
of a marriage setting,

00:04:29.108 --> 00:04:31.850
you know, things that go on in marriages.

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:33.420
People normally, you know,

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:35.970
try to keep it behind closed doors,

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:36.860
but they don't know, you know.

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:39.380
Are we in the bottom quartile of marriages

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:40.990
or are we in the top
quartile of marriages?

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:43.061
Do we argue an average amount?

00:04:43.061 --> 00:04:44.800
So I think there's
something interesting there.

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:47.110
It could be with parents and children.

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:49.490
You know, is this level of angst

00:04:49.490 --> 00:04:51.440
that we're experiencing
normal or is it isn't?

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:53.920
But if every parent was rating it,

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:55.060
maybe there's something interesting there.

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:56.050
So, that's one idea.

00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:57.670
It's kind of a fun idea
but I actually think

00:04:57.670 --> 00:05:00.200
it could have legs behind it. (laughs)

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:05.200
I think there could be
interesting apps around

00:05:05.637 --> 00:05:10.060
just understanding yourself better.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:12.240
You've seen kind of these
personality type apps,

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:14.630
but I think things
could, there is research

00:05:14.630 --> 00:05:15.850
and mental health research and things

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:18.010
about activities or things that you can do

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:21.190
that will improve your wellbeing.

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:22.710
I think there could be interesting apps

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:25.560
for folks to help each other either

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:27.160
with some type of volunteer work,

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:29.000
or help tutor each other.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:31.000
So those are the types of spaces

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.500
that I'd be interested in
if I were making an app.

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:35.323
So, let's see.

00:05:35.323 --> 00:05:38.740
From YouTube, Kate Grafe says
as a pre-service teacher,

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:40.630
I was wondering how to
implement collaborative learning

00:05:40.630 --> 00:05:41.463
through remote learning,

00:05:41.463 --> 00:05:42.910
and what does classroom
management look like

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:44.190
in this scenario?

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:45.440
It's a great question, Kate.

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:47.490
And the world is figuring it out,

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:49.490
and I won't claim to be the expert here.

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:52.200
I've actually worked
with a couple of teachers

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:53.270
in a couple of school districts trying

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:54.210
to figure it out myself.

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:56.620
I've tried to run a few sessions myself.

00:05:56.620 --> 00:05:58.620
The only tips I could give you based

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:01.550
on my limited experience
here with running real,

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:03.180
live virtual sessions

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:05.740
are the more interaction
you can get, the better.

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:07.130
I think, as you can imagine,

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:08.380
that's always the best practice,

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:09.340
even if you're in person.

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:12.440
But especially if you're
on a Google Meet, or Skype,

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:15.236
or Zoom session, it's
that much more important

00:06:15.236 --> 00:06:19.680
to pull out responses from
the people who are there.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.730
Because if you don't do that,
it's essentially equivalent

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:22.640
to a video.

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:25.020
And then you might as well be a video.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:26.920
So I would say try to
pick out as much responses

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:30.350
as possible, depending on
what platform you are using.

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:33.000
There are techniques,
you can mute everyone

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.030
so you don't get all of their
home noise and all of that.

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:36.277
And then you can call on people, say,

00:06:36.277 --> 00:06:38.040
"Well, what do you think?"

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:39.700
I know that Zoom has gallery view,

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:41.592
which is a really nice way of seeing,

00:06:41.592 --> 00:06:44.884
if you can fit everyone on one screen,

00:06:44.884 --> 00:06:48.100
you can say, Okay, does
anyone have a question?

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:49.330
You know, how do you feel about this?

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:51.640
You can say, how many of
you support the proposition?

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:52.880
How many of you are against it?

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:54.450
And I think that type of interaction

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:56.692
really keeps the conversation going.

00:06:56.692 --> 00:06:59.360
One thing that I do, I do this
on the weekend with friends,

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:01.050
where we try to talk about just, you know,

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:02.750
how do we become better people?

00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:05.790
When I finish the sessions,
and I'm usually the moderator,

00:07:05.790 --> 00:07:08.470
I go through all of the
tiles on Zoom and I make sure

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:09.920
that I get a response from everyone.

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:12.490
I'm just like, hey, everyone,
30 seconds, one minute,

00:07:12.490 --> 00:07:13.640
what's your takeaway from today?

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:16.205
How are you going to try to be different

00:07:16.205 --> 00:07:18.600
or observe things
differently over the course

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:19.460
of the next week?

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:22.340
So those are practices that
I've seen work reasonably well

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:25.603
in a Zoom or Google Meet type scenario.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:29.382
- Sal, you mentioned yesterday
about kind of approaches

00:07:29.382 --> 00:07:32.310
for kids who are either afraid of math

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:35.410
or are scared about tackling that subject.

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:36.860
We have a related question.

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:38.508
You talked about a process,
but we have a question

00:07:38.508 --> 00:07:43.190
from YouTube from ShockBlastYT.

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:45.543
Hi Sal, what's your
opinion on growth mindset?

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:49.050
Yeah, a good question ShockBlast,

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:50.120
and for those of you who don't know,

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:52.732
growth mindset's this idea that you,

00:07:52.732 --> 00:07:55.310
many times folks will
have a growth mindset

00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:56.370
or a fixed mindset.

00:07:56.370 --> 00:08:00.150
A fixed mindset is, you think
you either have the ability

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:00.983
or you don't.

00:08:00.983 --> 00:08:02.857
Let's say in math, you
say I'm a math person,

00:08:02.857 --> 00:08:04.870
I'm gifted in math.

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:06.375
Or I'm not gifted in math, I'm not.

00:08:06.375 --> 00:08:07.947
That's a fixed mindset either way.

00:08:07.947 --> 00:08:09.900
Whether you have a positive fixed mindset

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:11.440
or a negative fixed mindset.

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:14.240
A growth mindset doesn't
say I'm either good or bad

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:15.990
at something, a growth mindset would say,

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:19.150
well, I don't know how my capabilities,

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:20.930
the only way I'm able to push the envelope

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:23.410
is if I am willing to try things,

00:08:23.410 --> 00:08:25.410
I'm willing to step
out of my comfort zone,

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:27.640
and I'm willing to fail
every now and then.

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:30.370
And when I fail, I don't just
try to run away from that,

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:31.840
I reflect on why I failed

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:33.470
and how I could do it better next time.

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:35.680
And then I can keep going.

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:38.570
And as you can imagine, research has shown

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:40.570
is that folks with growth mindsets,

00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:42.960
there's a strong correlation
between growth mindsets

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:45.310
and people who are
successful in the field.

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:46.850
And you might think that, okay,

00:08:46.850 --> 00:08:49.202
I could get how the under-confident,
fixed mindset people,

00:08:49.202 --> 00:08:52.390
they're kind of psyching themselves out.

00:08:52.390 --> 00:08:53.960
Oh, I'm not good at this
subject, I'll never be,

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:55.080
so they don't even try.

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:57.110
But even the confident, or even,

00:08:57.110 --> 00:08:59.430
let's call it the
over-confident fixed mindsets

00:08:59.430 --> 00:09:00.360
also don't do well.

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:02.500
So if you say, in your head you're like,

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:05.510
I am genetically a great math person.

00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:06.950
What happens with a lot of those folks

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:08.990
is when they are faced with a challenge,

00:09:08.990 --> 00:09:11.640
they don't want a data
point that might undermine

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:13.280
their self perception.

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:16.560
So they actually might not
tackle the hard problem.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:17.393
Because it's like, well,

00:09:17.393 --> 00:09:18.700
what if I get the hard problem wrong?

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:20.770
Or what if my friends
see that I can't do it?

00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:23.130
Then that's gonna undermine
my self perception.

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:23.963
But then if you're not willing

00:09:23.963 --> 00:09:25.320
to try that more difficult thing,

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:26.520
you're never going to grow,

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:28.120
and in the end you're
not going to do as well

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:31.330
as a person who said, I'm just
always going to try things,

00:09:31.330 --> 00:09:33.150
and I'm gonna try to
tackle it and if I fail,

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:34.740
I'm gonna see how it goes.

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:37.170
And not only is there a strong correlation

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:38.940
between growth mindset
and success in life.

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:40.700
And I gotta say, that
everyone that I know in life

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:43.960
who have done well in a
domain has a growth mindset

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:45.530
about that domain.

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:47.926
But there's exercises or
things you can realize

00:09:47.926 --> 00:09:50.150
to build your growth mindset.

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:52.400
When you realize that your
brain is like a muscle,

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:54.344
the more you use it the stronger it gets.

00:09:54.344 --> 00:09:57.510
When you get questions
wrong and reflect on it,

00:09:57.510 --> 00:09:58.950
that's actually some of the best ways

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:01.188
to form neural connections.

00:10:01.188 --> 00:10:05.220
That actually helps you
build a growth mindset.

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:07.100
And so, the way it's
related to all the work

00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:09.260
at Khan Academy, you know,
a lot of what we talk

00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:11.920
about Khan Academy is the
notion of having as many shots

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:14.610
at goal as necessary when
you go practice exercises

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:15.630
on Khan Academy.

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:17.630
It's not like you just do five exercises

00:10:17.630 --> 00:10:20.740
and you got a 80% and we move
you on to the next thing.

00:10:20.740 --> 00:10:23.370
You can take that quiz, that unit test,

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:25.260
that course challenge,
that mastery challenge,

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:26.630
as many times as you want.

00:10:26.630 --> 00:10:31.440
And our team has painstakingly
put many items there,

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:34.410
so you're unlikely to
get repeat questions.

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:35.830
So you can get as much
practice and you can go

00:10:35.830 --> 00:10:39.420
from a 60%, 70%, 80%, to 100% percent.

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:41.990
And our view is the
notion of mastery learning

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:44.330
and growth mindset are hand in hand.

00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:47.010
A fixed mindset world
is I got a C on a test,

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:50.560
I guess I'm just a C student,
I'll move on with my life.

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:52.140
While a growth mindset would say,

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:54.880
I got a C on a test, let me try again.

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:56.460
And that's essentially mastery learning.

00:10:56.460 --> 00:11:01.230
Let me try to turn that
into an 85%, a 90%, a 95%.

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:05.170
So it's something we hold
very dear to Khan Academy,

00:11:05.170 --> 00:11:07.840
and I also think it's a
very important mindset.

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:10.270
And I have to be frank, we
all have growth mindsets,

00:11:10.270 --> 00:11:11.710
hopefully, in certain domains in our life

00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:13.100
and in other domains of our life,

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:15.590
for better, for worse, we
probably develop fixed mindsets,

00:11:15.590 --> 00:11:16.680
and it's good to be reflective

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:18.940
on where we do have those fixed mindsets,

00:11:18.940 --> 00:11:21.593
and how we can deprogram
ourselves a little bit.

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:24.040
So let's see.

00:11:24.040 --> 00:11:25.862
There's a question from Elliot H.

00:11:25.862 --> 00:11:28.816
Elliot, it feels like we're
getting to know each other.

00:11:28.816 --> 00:11:30.320
(laughs) You ask good questions.

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:31.930
You always refer to me as Dumbledore Sal,

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:33.940
which I find very flattering.

00:11:33.940 --> 00:11:35.770
You emphasize breaks and not overloading,

00:11:35.770 --> 00:11:38.600
yet you did just the opposite as a student

00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:39.750
and a young man.

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:42.170
Is this do as I say, not as I do?

00:11:42.170 --> 00:11:46.280
How much must one work to
be an innovator like you?

00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:47.280
All right, that's good.

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:50.700
I like questions that point
out potential hypocrisy

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:52.340
on my part. (laughs)

00:11:52.340 --> 00:11:54.753
And that's or at least
perceived hypocrisy.

00:11:54.753 --> 00:11:58.133
So you're right on a certain level.

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:01.700
If I were to, at least
the narrative I have

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:06.700
of my own youth, is that when
I was in elementary school,

00:12:06.780 --> 00:12:09.940
middle school, I was a decent student.

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:12.080
I essentially did, for the most part,

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:14.880
what I was asked to do.

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:16.580
And then even my freshman year

00:12:16.580 --> 00:12:18.340
in high school I was a decent student.

00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:22.040
But I wasn't the student who was really,

00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:25.320
I guess you could say, gunning in a way.

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:27.480
But then in my sophomore year I started

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:30.450
to kind of wake up to the
realities of the world.

00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:32.720
I said, well, you know,
if I wanna be a little bit

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:34.729
more ambitious, I have
to apply myself more.

00:12:34.729 --> 00:12:37.310
It can't just be me kind of calling it in

00:12:37.310 --> 00:12:38.870
and just doing what's necessary.

00:12:38.870 --> 00:12:42.220
So that is, I did become
a little bit more serious.

00:12:42.220 --> 00:12:44.070
And anyone who knows me back then

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:47.400
knows that I actually wasn't
that serious of a person.

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:51.260
I like to believe I'm kind
of, I have a playfulness

00:12:51.260 --> 00:12:52.280
about things.

00:12:52.280 --> 00:12:53.668
But I got serious about my life.

00:12:53.668 --> 00:12:58.241
My mom was a single mother,
raised me and my sister.

00:12:58.241 --> 00:13:02.750
She worked as a cashier
at my uncle's food store

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:03.583
at that time.

00:13:03.583 --> 00:13:05.340
So she wasn't making a lot of money.

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:08.220
She was making around 16, $17,000 a year.

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:09.460
I was on free and reduced lunch.

00:13:09.460 --> 00:13:11.670
So I had a little bit
of a fire in my belly

00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:15.660
to say, hey, I wanna live
like some of my friends

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:17.960
whose parents were professionals,

00:13:17.960 --> 00:13:21.302
and they had health
insurance, they had houses.

00:13:21.302 --> 00:13:23.702
And so that was a little
bit of a fire in the belly.

00:13:23.702 --> 00:13:27.170
And I do, so, through
the end of high school

00:13:27.170 --> 00:13:29.830
and early college I did
always try to push myself.

00:13:29.830 --> 00:13:32.870
And I will admit, sometimes
I kept adding, adding,

00:13:32.870 --> 00:13:34.468
adding, and at points it got,

00:13:34.468 --> 00:13:37.350
maybe I pushed a little bit too hard.

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:39.459
And I'm guilty of that today too.

00:13:39.459 --> 00:13:41.270
Even with the work at Khan Academy.

00:13:41.270 --> 00:13:45.580
If anything, it's not that,
now at Khan Academy I'm like,

00:13:45.580 --> 00:13:47.370
oh, it looks all like it's all perfect,

00:13:47.370 --> 00:13:48.610
I'll just take a break.

00:13:48.610 --> 00:13:51.639
There's something about
the further you go,

00:13:51.639 --> 00:13:53.900
there's a little process in your brain

00:13:53.900 --> 00:13:56.699
that says, Sal, you can't relax now.

00:13:56.699 --> 00:13:58.730
Look at how much there is to do.

00:13:58.730 --> 00:14:00.780
And so you keep layering more and more.

00:14:00.780 --> 00:14:02.390
But then you also start to appreciate

00:14:02.390 --> 00:14:04.347
if you layer on a little bit too much,

00:14:04.347 --> 00:14:08.520
that stress, that
anxiety, the relationships

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:09.353
that you have.

00:14:09.353 --> 00:14:10.660
They can slowly break down.

00:14:10.660 --> 00:14:13.130
And if you're not super observant about

00:14:13.130 --> 00:14:15.200
how much you are redlining,

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:17.740
they can fully break, the engine can stop.

00:14:17.740 --> 00:14:19.890
And so one of the things
that I've learned,

00:14:19.890 --> 00:14:22.910
and in some ways you often
learn it through experience,

00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:24.240
but hopefully you don't have to learn

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:27.810
it fully through experience,
is yeah, it's fine.

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:30.810
And everyone has a
different redline point.

00:14:30.810 --> 00:14:32.480
You know, you gotta challenge yourself.

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:33.460
That's a growth mindset.

00:14:33.460 --> 00:14:35.523
Keep challenging yourself,
don't beat up on yourself

00:14:35.523 --> 00:14:36.495
if you fail.

00:14:36.495 --> 00:14:38.100
And keep challenging yourself.

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:39.723
But if at some point you feel like,

00:14:39.723 --> 00:14:42.617
wow, this is starting
to affect my wellbeing,

00:14:42.617 --> 00:14:44.591
it's starting to affect my relationships,

00:14:44.591 --> 00:14:49.591
I can't handle this level
of intensity for too long.

00:14:51.130 --> 00:14:55.040
That's a good sign for you to
take your foot off the pedal.

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:58.320
And in some circles it
can kind of be viewed

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:01.170
as a sign of weakness, that
oh, they're not so intense,

00:15:01.170 --> 00:15:02.003
et cetera, et cetera.

00:15:02.003 --> 00:15:05.360
But the reality is, anyone who
I've known who's done well,

00:15:05.360 --> 00:15:07.420
yes, they might push themselves,

00:15:07.420 --> 00:15:09.150
they have a growth mindset,
they're stepping out

00:15:09.150 --> 00:15:10.810
of their comfort zone,
they're willing to try things

00:15:10.810 --> 00:15:11.730
and fail.

00:15:11.730 --> 00:15:14.980
But they have a very good
sense that this is a marathon.

00:15:14.980 --> 00:15:16.330
The people who do well aren't the people

00:15:16.330 --> 00:15:19.160
who just pull five all-nighters in a row

00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:20.250
and then burn out.

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:22.667
They're the people who are able to keep

00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:26.870
whatever they're trying to achieve going

00:15:26.870 --> 00:15:28.610
for long periods of time.

00:15:28.610 --> 00:15:30.220
And that's something I remind myself

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:32.270
and that's what I would
remind to all of y'all.

00:15:32.270 --> 00:15:34.070
Figure out what your capacity is,

00:15:34.070 --> 00:15:37.500
what you can do, and actually
always leave a little bit

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:41.110
of that margin of safety,
that extra flex space

00:15:41.110 --> 00:15:42.310
to recharge.

00:15:42.310 --> 00:15:46.750
And then you'll be well
equipped for the marathon,

00:15:46.750 --> 00:15:48.394
so to speak.

00:15:48.394 --> 00:15:52.440
I have to think, even
in college, I was very,

00:15:54.070 --> 00:15:56.250
I probably called myself lazy at the time.

00:15:56.250 --> 00:15:58.500
But I did make a point of making sure

00:15:58.500 --> 00:15:59.880
that I had time with friends,

00:15:59.880 --> 00:16:01.920
that I was getting the
full college experience,

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:04.911
that I wasn't only just trying

00:16:04.911 --> 00:16:07.760
to optimize a grade or
something like that.

00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:09.910
I'm so glad I did it,
because frankly the things

00:16:09.910 --> 00:16:11.710
that I remember from college are much more

00:16:11.710 --> 00:16:13.440
of the connections I made,

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:15.890
the conversations I had with friends.

00:16:15.890 --> 00:16:17.260
My wife, I met in college.

00:16:17.260 --> 00:16:18.850
So that was a great outcome.

00:16:18.850 --> 00:16:21.680
And I don't remember a
lot of the classes I took

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:25.750
or whether I got an A
or A- or a B in a class.

00:16:25.750 --> 00:16:27.920
So there's definitely,
you need to make space

00:16:27.920 --> 00:16:29.643
for all of those aspects of life.

00:16:30.490 --> 00:16:32.000
But good question, and it's always good

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:36.330
to call people on whether
they're not being consistent

00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:38.123
in their advice with how they live.

00:16:39.270 --> 00:16:43.950
So from YouTube, Susana Garcia Dominguez,

00:16:43.950 --> 00:16:46.609
Susana I feel like I'm
getting to know you as well.

00:16:46.609 --> 00:16:48.180
(laughs) You're asking
a lot of good questions.

00:16:48.180 --> 00:16:49.640
In light of these health initiatives,

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:51.780
do you think elementary
school are getting too big?

00:16:51.780 --> 00:16:54.067
For example, schools that
are 400 plus students.

00:16:54.067 --> 00:16:55.920
So, I'm not sure, I don't know.

00:16:55.920 --> 00:16:58.560
I'm not an expert on, I mean,

00:16:58.560 --> 00:16:59.950
based on your question,
it sounds like you might

00:16:59.950 --> 00:17:01.990
be asking like the epidemiology of it.

00:17:01.990 --> 00:17:04.470
And if you put more
than 400 kids in a room,

00:17:04.470 --> 00:17:06.990
are they going to be spreading
diseases and whatnot.

00:17:06.990 --> 00:17:07.823
I don't know.

00:17:07.823 --> 00:17:10.400
I don't know, my intuition there

00:17:10.400 --> 00:17:11.770
is it's less about the number,

00:17:11.770 --> 00:17:14.427
it's more about how the
school is structured.

00:17:14.427 --> 00:17:17.782
But I would say, generally speaking,

00:17:17.782 --> 00:17:20.570
it is nice the more attention,

00:17:20.570 --> 00:17:23.047
especially young kids can
get, supports from parents,

00:17:23.047 --> 00:17:25.870
and students and teachers especially.

00:17:25.870 --> 00:17:30.350
So yeah, I could imagine
as classes get too large,

00:17:30.350 --> 00:17:33.650
that that can be not ideal
for kid's development

00:17:33.650 --> 00:17:36.510
or feeling supported and things like that.

00:17:36.510 --> 00:17:40.120
So from YouTube, Miss
Melissa Gaudreau asked,

00:17:40.120 --> 00:17:42.010
what is the best way to assess students

00:17:42.010 --> 00:17:44.550
during this time of remote learning?

00:17:44.550 --> 00:17:47.740
I'm well aware that students cheat.

00:17:47.740 --> 00:17:52.117
So it depends what you're
trying to do the assessment for.

00:17:53.140 --> 00:17:55.030
We've actually had
conversation at Khan Academy,

00:17:55.030 --> 00:17:57.230
and I've had conversations
with other groups

00:17:57.230 --> 00:17:59.410
that are trying to
assess student learning.

00:17:59.410 --> 00:18:02.700
So Khan Academy is
actually a very good way,

00:18:02.700 --> 00:18:04.626
if you wanna just
understand where either you

00:18:04.626 --> 00:18:06.250
as the student, where you are,

00:18:06.250 --> 00:18:08.460
or if you have a child or you're a teacher

00:18:08.460 --> 00:18:10.090
and you have students.

00:18:10.090 --> 00:18:12.650
Have them work on the course
challenge on Khan Academy.

00:18:12.650 --> 00:18:14.345
Do the unit tests on Khan Academy.

00:18:14.345 --> 00:18:16.410
Or just say hey, get as far as you can

00:18:16.410 --> 00:18:18.770
in your grade level of
course, or whatever you need

00:18:18.770 --> 00:18:20.350
to work on in Khan Academy.

00:18:20.350 --> 00:18:22.310
And that level of mastery,

00:18:22.310 --> 00:18:23.700
that percent completion that they are,

00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:25.730
that's a really good
assessment of where they are

00:18:25.730 --> 00:18:26.980
in their learning path.

00:18:26.980 --> 00:18:28.660
We've done multiple studies

00:18:28.660 --> 00:18:31.210
that show very strong
correlation between progress

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:33.750
on Khan Academy, mastery on Khan Academy,

00:18:33.750 --> 00:18:36.670
and other third party
benchmark assessments.

00:18:36.670 --> 00:18:39.570
So if you just wanna get a
read of where someone is,

00:18:39.570 --> 00:18:41.750
I think you have the tools.

00:18:41.750 --> 00:18:43.700
If you want, if you're thinking about

00:18:43.700 --> 00:18:45.610
how do I give someone a grade?

00:18:45.610 --> 00:18:47.610
Or how do I give them a score

00:18:47.610 --> 00:18:49.880
that might impact college admissions

00:18:49.880 --> 00:18:52.360
or give them a credential of some kind?

00:18:52.360 --> 00:18:54.390
Then the stakes to get higher,

00:18:54.390 --> 00:18:56.650
and to your point, there's
some subset of folks

00:18:56.650 --> 00:18:58.902
who might be tempted to
cheat in that scenario.

00:18:58.902 --> 00:19:02.430
I think there's ways to
minimize that cheating.

00:19:02.430 --> 00:19:04.340
Obviously cheating can
happen in any scenario.

00:19:04.340 --> 00:19:07.150
When you go to a, there's
classes I remember in college

00:19:07.150 --> 00:19:08.480
where you can go to the gym

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:10.330
and there's three hundred
people taking a test,

00:19:10.330 --> 00:19:11.930
they never checked our IDs.

00:19:11.930 --> 00:19:14.080
So if someone wanted to cheat, they could.

00:19:14.080 --> 00:19:17.560
But you often hear they're
really just cheating themselves.

00:19:17.560 --> 00:19:19.210
But I think in this online world,

00:19:20.500 --> 00:19:22.930
I'm a big believer, actually,
in kind of honor codes.

00:19:22.930 --> 00:19:25.292
You tell people, look, sign this document,

00:19:25.292 --> 00:19:27.860
vow that you will not cheat.

00:19:27.860 --> 00:19:29.770
We have you on video saying that.

00:19:29.770 --> 00:19:32.100
And if you cheat, and if it's
discovered you're cheating,

00:19:32.100 --> 00:19:33.480
it's proved you're cheating,

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:34.830
the penalties are very high.

00:19:34.830 --> 00:19:36.330
So I think that's one way to do it.

00:19:36.330 --> 00:19:39.630
Another way is, I think you
could do some light proctoring,

00:19:39.630 --> 00:19:42.640
even in the socially distanced world.

00:19:42.640 --> 00:19:44.290
Where you say, okay, everyone,

00:19:44.290 --> 00:19:46.920
if you wanna get credit for this thing,

00:19:46.920 --> 00:19:48.464
maybe you use Khan Academy,

00:19:48.464 --> 00:19:51.650
come onto Zoom or Google Meet on this day,

00:19:51.650 --> 00:19:53.160
and I will proctor you all.

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:55.770
And I will kind of observe
you as you're taking this.

00:19:55.770 --> 00:19:58.290
Now, it's not gonna be
completely foolproof.

00:19:58.290 --> 00:20:02.830
There might be, a student
might have a sibling off camera

00:20:02.830 --> 00:20:04.851
whispering into their ear or something.

00:20:04.851 --> 00:20:07.830
But there's no such thing as foolproof.

00:20:07.830 --> 00:20:11.300
You really just wanna get a 99% solution.

00:20:11.300 --> 00:20:14.150
And I think, you don't want
perfect to be the enemy of good.

00:20:14.150 --> 00:20:16.570
I believe most students
wanna do what's right,

00:20:16.570 --> 00:20:17.560
they don't wanna cheat.

00:20:17.560 --> 00:20:18.900
And especially even the ones that might

00:20:18.900 --> 00:20:23.400
be a little bit more ethically
ambiguous (laughs) about it,

00:20:23.400 --> 00:20:24.685
or ambivalent about it.

00:20:24.685 --> 00:20:29.097
If you have some penalties if
they're discovered cheating.

00:20:29.097 --> 00:20:32.230
We had David Coleman
on about the AP tests,

00:20:32.230 --> 00:20:33.420
and I think they're taking that route.

00:20:33.420 --> 00:20:35.440
They are taking a lot
of security measures.

00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:36.620
But at the end of the day,

00:20:36.620 --> 00:20:38.690
if a student is discovered cheating,

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:42.270
plagiarizing, whatever,
there's severe repercussions

00:20:42.270 --> 00:20:43.830
for them in life.

00:20:43.830 --> 00:20:45.480
And hopefully some of what happened

00:20:45.480 --> 00:20:47.030
with the Varsity Blues scandal,

00:20:47.030 --> 00:20:48.794
with these college admissions scandals,

00:20:48.794 --> 00:20:51.900
its shown people the
downsides of even attempting

00:20:51.900 --> 00:20:54.418
to cheat are pretty large.

00:20:54.418 --> 00:20:57.702
Ideally your ethics,
yourself, your consciousness,

00:20:57.702 --> 00:21:00.500
your conscientiousness
keeps you from cheating.

00:21:00.500 --> 00:21:02.939
But if you're afraid of outcomes,

00:21:02.939 --> 00:21:06.043
(laughs) then keep in
mind it could be bad.

00:21:07.300 --> 00:21:08.133
So, let's see--

00:21:08.133 --> 00:21:10.521
- Sal, we have a pretty
good question for you, Sal.

00:21:10.521 --> 00:21:12.360
From YouTube, Presheda Jane asks,

00:21:12.360 --> 00:21:15.660
Hi Sal, what factors of
ed-tech needs to change

00:21:15.660 --> 00:21:17.580
to help in situations like these?

00:21:17.580 --> 00:21:20.393
Especially to help kids who
have no access to school?

00:21:22.310 --> 00:21:23.670
- Yeah, that's a great question.

00:21:23.670 --> 00:21:26.000
The big one, the obvious one is access,

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:28.103
as your question implies.

00:21:28.103 --> 00:21:30.305
That's not something
that we at Khan Academy

00:21:30.305 --> 00:21:32.067
have been directly been able to do.

00:21:32.067 --> 00:21:35.370
Obviously we can't source laptops

00:21:35.370 --> 00:21:37.080
and give people free internet access.

00:21:37.080 --> 00:21:37.913
It's not what we do.

00:21:37.913 --> 00:21:40.850
We are dependent on that somehow existing.

00:21:40.850 --> 00:21:43.320
What we've been seeing
because of the COVID crisis,

00:21:43.320 --> 00:21:46.130
in schools, especially
in places like the US,

00:21:46.130 --> 00:21:47.810
schools have actually
done a really good job

00:21:47.810 --> 00:21:48.970
over the last 10 years,

00:21:48.970 --> 00:21:51.130
and there have been government
programs like E-Rate,

00:21:51.130 --> 00:21:53.480
which have made internet access

00:21:53.480 --> 00:21:56.918
and devices much more
common in public schools.

00:21:56.918 --> 00:22:00.640
What this crisis highlights
is the inequity at home

00:22:00.640 --> 00:22:03.170
is now a major issue.

00:22:03.170 --> 00:22:06.470
It's always been an issue, but
this really makes it stark.

00:22:06.470 --> 00:22:08.300
And it's not just an
inequity to access something

00:22:08.300 --> 00:22:10.416
like Khan Academy, it's also an inequity

00:22:10.416 --> 00:22:13.520
in terms of, in this time
of social distancing,

00:22:13.520 --> 00:22:16.010
just to stay sane you
wanna be able to connect

00:22:16.010 --> 00:22:18.650
with friends and family over a hang out,

00:22:18.650 --> 00:22:19.660
over a Zoom session.

00:22:19.660 --> 00:22:21.040
And if you don't have internet access,

00:22:21.040 --> 00:22:22.320
it's going to be very, very difficult

00:22:22.320 --> 00:22:24.940
to just feel connected
to others right now.

00:22:24.940 --> 00:22:27.890
We're seeing some really
good programs out there.

00:22:27.890 --> 00:22:30.170
One of our guests that we're
gonna have later this week

00:22:30.170 --> 00:22:33.200
is Ray Dalio, Ray and Barbara Dalio.

00:22:33.200 --> 00:22:36.430
They have sponsored 60,000 laptops

00:22:36.430 --> 00:22:39.060
for low income high school
students in Connecticut.

00:22:39.060 --> 00:22:40.990
And they're working with
the telecom providers

00:22:40.990 --> 00:22:42.530
to get them free internet.

00:22:42.530 --> 00:22:46.150
We had Dr. Jesus Jara
on from Clark County,

00:22:46.150 --> 00:22:48.890
which is greater Las Vegas,
the 5th largest school district

00:22:48.890 --> 00:22:49.760
in the country.

00:22:49.760 --> 00:22:52.013
They did a really great job
of first distributing laptops

00:22:52.013 --> 00:22:55.070
to their seniors, because those students

00:22:55.070 --> 00:22:56.470
that it's most mission critical.

00:22:56.470 --> 00:22:58.564
And then they got another 40,000 laptops

00:22:58.564 --> 00:22:59.960
that they were able to distribute

00:22:59.960 --> 00:23:01.320
to all of the students who needed it.

00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:03.510
And they had partnerships
with the local cable companies

00:23:03.510 --> 00:23:05.190
to get free internet access.

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:08.740
One of our partners at
Khan Academy, Comcast,

00:23:08.740 --> 00:23:11.550
is offering free internet
access to students

00:23:11.550 --> 00:23:14.080
for the next, through the crisis.

00:23:14.080 --> 00:23:16.790
So the good thing is, a
lot of actors, I believe,

00:23:16.790 --> 00:23:18.870
are doing the right thing.

00:23:18.870 --> 00:23:22.340
My hope is that this doesn't just happen

00:23:22.340 --> 00:23:23.600
through this crisis,

00:23:23.600 --> 00:23:26.110
that the crisis is the catalyst
that makes everyone think

00:23:26.110 --> 00:23:27.480
very seriously about it.

00:23:27.480 --> 00:23:29.560
But then as we exit the
crisis people realize

00:23:29.560 --> 00:23:32.430
that it's not a just a
nice to have for students

00:23:32.430 --> 00:23:33.720
to have internet access,

00:23:33.720 --> 00:23:35.140
or for anyone to have internet access.

00:23:35.140 --> 00:23:38.540
It really is approaching
a modern human right,

00:23:38.540 --> 00:23:40.840
like clean drinking water or shelter.

00:23:40.840 --> 00:23:43.394
And if you get that part solved,

00:23:43.394 --> 00:23:46.530
then I think ed-tech goes into,

00:23:46.530 --> 00:23:48.200
okay, how do we make sure that students

00:23:48.200 --> 00:23:49.250
can stay engaged?

00:23:49.250 --> 00:23:52.550
How do we connect what
happens in ed-tech or online

00:23:52.550 --> 00:23:54.850
and make sure that it
works and it's integrated

00:23:54.850 --> 00:23:56.290
and it's cohesive with what can happen

00:23:56.290 --> 00:23:58.400
in the physical environment,
in the physical classroom?

00:23:58.400 --> 00:24:00.540
I don't do these things in
tension with each other,

00:24:00.540 --> 00:24:02.904
I think online should be
able to seamlessly integrate

00:24:02.904 --> 00:24:05.795
in the physical classroom,
but in times of crisis,

00:24:05.795 --> 00:24:08.420
you can then lean, like we're
going through right now,

00:24:08.420 --> 00:24:10.570
you can lean more heavily on the online.

00:24:10.570 --> 00:24:12.620
I think there's interesting
questions like we just had

00:24:12.620 --> 00:24:15.380
about how can things
like assessment occur,

00:24:15.380 --> 00:24:17.750
credentialing occur online?

00:24:17.750 --> 00:24:21.310
Especially when maybe there's
a larger fear of cheating.

00:24:21.310 --> 00:24:23.540
How do you connect online experiences

00:24:23.540 --> 00:24:27.030
and mastery achieved online
to real opportunities?

00:24:27.030 --> 00:24:29.220
So I think all of these
are interesting frontiers.

00:24:29.220 --> 00:24:31.170
I'm also fascinated, you know,
we had that earlier question

00:24:31.170 --> 00:24:32.890
about apps to create.

00:24:32.890 --> 00:24:36.930
I'm fascinated by this
idea of leveraging online.

00:24:36.930 --> 00:24:38.340
You know, a lot of Khan Academy right now

00:24:38.340 --> 00:24:40.750
you could view as
asynchronous which means,

00:24:40.750 --> 00:24:42.980
everyone's learning at
their own time and pace.

00:24:42.980 --> 00:24:44.700
But one of the dreams for Khan Academy,

00:24:44.700 --> 00:24:46.370
and I wrote this in
"One World Schoolhouse,"

00:24:46.370 --> 00:24:48.600
are there ways to connect
people who are willing

00:24:48.600 --> 00:24:50.770
to tutor or willing to teach a subject,

00:24:50.770 --> 00:24:52.320
and people who need that support?

00:24:52.320 --> 00:24:53.980
It's great that we have
videos, it's great that we

00:24:53.980 --> 00:24:56.040
have as much practice
and feedback as you need.

00:24:56.040 --> 00:24:58.010
It's great that you have
hints and get the solution

00:24:58.010 --> 00:24:58.920
of any problem.

00:24:58.920 --> 00:25:00.970
But we know that you still might be stuck,

00:25:00.970 --> 00:25:03.200
or you still might need
kind of a pick-me-up

00:25:03.200 --> 00:25:04.320
or get a little bit motivated.

00:25:04.320 --> 00:25:07.760
And it's nothing like an
amazing teacher or tutor

00:25:07.760 --> 00:25:10.300
or just someone who cares to help you out

00:25:10.300 --> 00:25:11.133
of that moment.

00:25:11.133 --> 00:25:12.290
So I would love to leverage.

00:25:12.290 --> 00:25:14.530
I'm always fascinated by
how to leverage technology

00:25:14.530 --> 00:25:18.143
to actually create more
richness of human interactions.

00:25:20.340 --> 00:25:24.130
So let's see, from Facebook,
Kevin Rodriguez asks,

00:25:24.130 --> 00:25:25.900
hey Sal, are there any
plans of adding topics

00:25:25.900 --> 00:25:28.980
about environmental education, renewables

00:25:28.980 --> 00:25:30.740
and climate change or
sustainable development

00:25:30.740 --> 00:25:31.800
on Khan Academy?

00:25:31.800 --> 00:25:33.600
Many of us in the youth climate movement

00:25:33.600 --> 00:25:35.900
would be delighted to help out with that.

00:25:35.900 --> 00:25:38.310
Kevin, it's a great
question, and it's a topic

00:25:38.310 --> 00:25:41.230
that we've talked about a
lot here at Khan Academy.

00:25:41.230 --> 00:25:42.770
You know, one of the
interesting challenges

00:25:42.770 --> 00:25:45.440
is there's topics that are very current

00:25:45.440 --> 00:25:49.860
and very relevant, but if
we create an amazing course

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:52.194
on that, you know, we would try.

00:25:52.194 --> 00:25:54.760
If you don't connect it,

00:25:54.760 --> 00:25:56.240
and this goes to the previous answer,

00:25:56.240 --> 00:25:58.153
if you don't connect it
somehow to what's going on

00:25:58.153 --> 00:26:01.014
through student's
academics, their schools,

00:26:01.014 --> 00:26:05.320
credentials, et cetera, it
might not be used by everyone.

00:26:05.320 --> 00:26:07.010
And so what we're trying to figure out is,

00:26:07.010 --> 00:26:11.010
are there places, say in
science, middle school science

00:26:11.010 --> 00:26:13.500
for example, where these
really important ideas

00:26:13.500 --> 00:26:16.530
about environmental
science could find a home,

00:26:16.530 --> 00:26:20.320
and people can understand
the world through that lens

00:26:20.320 --> 00:26:24.010
of critical thinking,
through the lens of science,

00:26:24.010 --> 00:26:25.660
I think could be really interesting.

00:26:25.660 --> 00:26:28.360
So we actually are, we
have been soliciting

00:26:28.360 --> 00:26:31.270
from potential philanthropists
if folks wanted to fund

00:26:31.270 --> 00:26:32.500
that type of content.

00:26:32.500 --> 00:26:34.940
Middle school level, high
school level, a science.

00:26:34.940 --> 00:26:37.130
Amgen has been a great partner of ours

00:26:37.130 --> 00:26:39.280
sponsoring content at the high school

00:26:39.280 --> 00:26:40.840
and early college level science.

00:26:40.840 --> 00:26:43.940
Things like biology and physics.

00:26:43.940 --> 00:26:45.825
But we're looking for
partners who can help sponsor

00:26:45.825 --> 00:26:48.410
some of that content at that middle school

00:26:48.410 --> 00:26:50.060
or even elementary school level as well.

00:26:50.060 --> 00:26:51.330
Which I think could touch on a lot of

00:26:51.330 --> 00:26:53.423
what you just talked about.

00:26:54.500 --> 00:26:58.410
So let's see, from
Facebook Stacy Rosenbaum

00:26:58.410 --> 00:27:00.530
asking a similar question,
I'd like to see more science

00:27:00.530 --> 00:27:02.930
and social studies on the
elementary end of the spectrum.

00:27:02.930 --> 00:27:04.308
We would too, Stacy.

00:27:04.308 --> 00:27:06.227
And so the simple answer is,

00:27:06.227 --> 00:27:10.260
we're always looking for funding for folks

00:27:10.260 --> 00:27:12.203
who might be interested in sponsoring

00:27:12.203 --> 00:27:15.400
those types of courses.

00:27:15.400 --> 00:27:18.250
So let us know, forward any folks.

00:27:18.250 --> 00:27:21.050
And sometimes we dig in
reserves to try to build out

00:27:21.050 --> 00:27:22.070
these courses, regardless,

00:27:22.070 --> 00:27:23.660
and hope that someone will fund it.

00:27:23.660 --> 00:27:25.720
But these are definitely
the types of courses

00:27:25.720 --> 00:27:28.660
that we do want to add on Khan Academy.

00:27:28.660 --> 00:27:31.240
So let's see, other questions here.

00:27:31.240 --> 00:27:36.240
I have a question from
Laura Verdeen on YouTube,

00:27:38.920 --> 00:27:40.190
you helped me not only pass,

00:27:40.190 --> 00:27:41.980
but do a great job on my
college physics courses.

00:27:41.980 --> 00:27:43.250
Oh, that's good to hear, Laura.

00:27:43.250 --> 00:27:46.510
How did you learn to
explain concepts so well?

00:27:46.510 --> 00:27:49.863
Well, thank you that you found it useful.

00:27:51.300 --> 00:27:56.300
You know, what I think,
and I don't know for sure,

00:27:56.450 --> 00:27:58.775
if folks are resonating
with content that I

00:27:58.775 --> 00:28:00.949
or other team members have created,

00:28:00.949 --> 00:28:05.521
I try to think out loud when I do videos.

00:28:05.521 --> 00:28:08.930
And I try to be very
transparent with my thoughts.

00:28:08.930 --> 00:28:11.400
I mean, even in this
ask-me-anything forum,

00:28:11.400 --> 00:28:13.540
hopefully you can tell
I'm being very transparent

00:28:13.540 --> 00:28:14.373
with my thoughts.

00:28:14.373 --> 00:28:16.580
I'm not being machiavellian,
trying to say,

00:28:16.580 --> 00:28:17.940
well, what do people want me to say,

00:28:17.940 --> 00:28:20.220
and what is going to be the
most polished professional,

00:28:20.220 --> 00:28:22.420
I'm like, yeah, okay Sal,
that's probably the truth,

00:28:22.420 --> 00:28:23.490
just share it with folks.

00:28:23.490 --> 00:28:26.830
And I try to do the same
thing when I'm teaching

00:28:26.830 --> 00:28:28.240
on Khan Academy.

00:28:28.240 --> 00:28:30.359
I think a lot of times if
you look at a textbook,

00:28:30.359 --> 00:28:32.415
or even sometimes in a lecture,

00:28:32.415 --> 00:28:35.308
it all seems scripted and very perfect.

00:28:35.308 --> 00:28:37.910
But it's not exactly how people think.

00:28:37.910 --> 00:28:39.298
And often times it'll skip steps,

00:28:39.298 --> 00:28:42.669
it doesn't connect it to
what you might already know.

00:28:42.669 --> 00:28:45.890
And when I try to do
something on Khan Academy,

00:28:45.890 --> 00:28:47.610
my brain, while I'm doing the video,

00:28:47.610 --> 00:28:48.667
is trying to do all of those things.

00:28:48.667 --> 00:28:51.550
And some of those insights
are things that my brain

00:28:51.550 --> 00:28:54.500
is just surfacing in real
time, I didn't plan on it.

00:28:54.500 --> 00:28:56.970
Just saying, hey, well this
is interesting that entropy

00:28:56.970 --> 00:28:59.230
in the computer science
information theory version

00:28:59.230 --> 00:29:01.940
is actually mathematically identical

00:29:01.940 --> 00:29:05.660
to entropy in the chemistry
kinetic theory ideas.

00:29:05.660 --> 00:29:07.640
What is it saying about information?

00:29:07.640 --> 00:29:09.820
You know, just trying to
draw these connections

00:29:09.820 --> 00:29:11.140
that just might happen in your mind.

00:29:11.140 --> 00:29:13.850
And I think, hopefully,
when people listen to that,

00:29:13.850 --> 00:29:16.030
they're like oh, this person
is actually here with me.

00:29:16.030 --> 00:29:18.470
Even though is it asynchronous,
we're not actually

00:29:18.470 --> 00:29:20.640
at the same time with each other.

00:29:20.640 --> 00:29:22.950
But you're like, oh, I can
kind of get into the mind.

00:29:22.950 --> 00:29:25.840
And I've seen some folks
who are making lessons,

00:29:25.840 --> 00:29:27.280
they want it to be so perfect,

00:29:27.280 --> 00:29:29.358
they script it out, they skip the steps,

00:29:29.358 --> 00:29:31.747
and they sound very
professional, they'll say,

00:29:31.747 --> 00:29:34.235
"The next step in this process
is when the mitochondria."

00:29:34.235 --> 00:29:37.190
But that's not how we talk in real life.

00:29:37.190 --> 00:29:41.710
And when you hear that kind of,
I say, GPS device type talk,

00:29:41.710 --> 00:29:44.160
our human brains don't
feel so connected to it.

00:29:44.160 --> 00:29:47.890
So I don't know, we don't know
if there is a secret sauce

00:29:47.890 --> 00:29:51.180
to Khan Academy, but that's
some of our intuitions,

00:29:51.180 --> 00:29:52.850
is that we try to keep it intuitive,

00:29:52.850 --> 00:29:55.330
we try to keep it very informal.

00:29:55.330 --> 00:29:59.220
When I make content, I
don't view it as I'm someone

00:29:59.220 --> 00:30:00.840
who knows knowledge, and you are someone

00:30:00.840 --> 00:30:02.120
who does not know knowledge.

00:30:02.120 --> 00:30:04.275
I view it as like, hey, we're both people

00:30:04.275 --> 00:30:06.270
and we are both equals,

00:30:06.270 --> 00:30:07.840
and we have to respect each other.

00:30:07.840 --> 00:30:10.140
And I might, right now,
just have a few insights

00:30:10.140 --> 00:30:11.820
that you don't have yet.

00:30:11.820 --> 00:30:14.540
And so I think whether you're
talking to a five year old

00:30:14.540 --> 00:30:17.940
or a 50 year old, to treat people with

00:30:17.940 --> 00:30:20.190
that kind of conversational,
don't talk down to them

00:30:20.190 --> 00:30:21.480
and also don't talk above them,

00:30:21.480 --> 00:30:22.760
I think also might help,

00:30:22.760 --> 00:30:24.500
and it de-stresses the interaction.

00:30:24.500 --> 00:30:27.072
Anyway, I don't know. (laughs)

00:30:27.072 --> 00:30:29.620
We do try to glean some of this.

00:30:29.620 --> 00:30:31.640
Because obviously as we grow
in scale and add more content,

00:30:31.640 --> 00:30:36.140
we do wanna only get better
at some of these dimensions

00:30:36.140 --> 00:30:37.620
for our content.

00:30:37.620 --> 00:30:39.670
So it looks like we're all out of time.

00:30:39.670 --> 00:30:42.170
This is super.

00:30:42.170 --> 00:30:43.290
Actually, I'm gonna
answer one more question,

00:30:43.290 --> 00:30:44.650
I just saw this, it
seems really interesting.

00:30:44.650 --> 00:30:45.610
Six Just One of Six, and Six,

00:30:45.610 --> 00:30:47.780
you've asked several
questions that were very good,

00:30:47.780 --> 00:30:48.613
thank you.

00:30:48.613 --> 00:30:50.020
What do you think we will
say when you look back

00:30:50.020 --> 00:30:51.160
at this time?

00:30:51.160 --> 00:30:52.360
And I think that's a great question

00:30:52.360 --> 00:30:55.050
to kind of finish today's session,

00:30:55.050 --> 00:30:56.563
today's livestream on.

00:30:57.866 --> 00:31:00.470
The time we live in, we
don't know how it's going

00:31:00.470 --> 00:31:01.950
to play out over the next few months,

00:31:01.950 --> 00:31:04.226
is for sure going to be one of those times

00:31:04.226 --> 00:31:07.750
that happens every few
decades, that people are going

00:31:07.750 --> 00:31:10.290
to be referring to for decades to come.

00:31:10.290 --> 00:31:12.210
We are all going to be
telling our children

00:31:12.210 --> 00:31:14.208
and other people's children,
and our children's children

00:31:14.208 --> 00:31:15.610
about this time.

00:31:15.610 --> 00:31:16.820
And our children, who are alive today,

00:31:16.820 --> 00:31:18.150
are going to tell their children about it.

00:31:18.150 --> 00:31:21.300
And they're going to talk
about how it changed society.

00:31:21.300 --> 00:31:23.640
You can go back to some
things like World War One,

00:31:23.640 --> 00:31:25.263
the Great Depression, World War Two,

00:31:25.263 --> 00:31:28.750
The Cold War, which is kind of
over a longer period of time.

00:31:28.750 --> 00:31:30.450
The Vietnam War, things like this.

00:31:30.450 --> 00:31:31.770
People all have memories of that

00:31:31.770 --> 00:31:34.589
and how it changed
society in certain ways.

00:31:34.589 --> 00:31:38.580
9/11 and I'm speaking from a
very kind of American centric

00:31:38.580 --> 00:31:40.570
point of view, but you can
imagine in other countries

00:31:40.570 --> 00:31:42.130
there were things, traumas,

00:31:42.130 --> 00:31:43.880
that their countries went through

00:31:43.880 --> 00:31:45.290
that had lasting implications.

00:31:45.290 --> 00:31:47.300
Many of them negative, many
of the lasting implications

00:31:47.300 --> 00:31:48.133
might have been positive.

00:31:48.133 --> 00:31:51.240
It might have been somehow
put systems in place

00:31:51.240 --> 00:31:53.790
so that these things
might not happen again.

00:31:53.790 --> 00:31:56.410
So I think we will, that
will be a lasting consequence

00:31:56.410 --> 00:31:57.666
in terms of what are those changes?

00:31:57.666 --> 00:31:59.910
I've talked about this
in other livestreams,

00:31:59.910 --> 00:32:03.700
I think it's making all of
us appreciate the things

00:32:03.700 --> 00:32:04.950
that really matter more.

00:32:04.950 --> 00:32:07.430
You know, we all get caught
up in the rat race of like,

00:32:07.430 --> 00:32:09.540
oh, I need to get into this
college and get that grade,

00:32:09.540 --> 00:32:11.720
and get this job, and
then I need this title

00:32:11.720 --> 00:32:13.870
and I need that office and
I need that type of car.

00:32:13.870 --> 00:32:15.800
And I could use a little
bit more square footage

00:32:15.800 --> 00:32:16.633
in my house.

00:32:16.633 --> 00:32:18.520
So then people will think
that I've really succeeded

00:32:18.520 --> 00:32:20.260
and I can dress a little better.

00:32:20.260 --> 00:32:22.060
And whether we admit it or not,

00:32:22.060 --> 00:32:23.880
all of us, to some degree,

00:32:23.880 --> 00:32:25.540
get a little bit caught up in that.

00:32:25.540 --> 00:32:28.400
Some of us, to a large
degree, get caught up in that.

00:32:28.400 --> 00:32:30.220
And I think when
everything just comes down

00:32:30.220 --> 00:32:33.160
to your health, how do
you protect your family,

00:32:33.160 --> 00:32:34.682
how do you protect society?

00:32:34.682 --> 00:32:37.773
There's people who've lost their jobs,

00:32:37.773 --> 00:32:41.470
they don't know how they're
going to buy food next week.

00:32:41.470 --> 00:32:44.630
It all, I think it's
sobering and can be sobering

00:32:44.630 --> 00:32:46.060
in a healthy way that makes you realize

00:32:46.060 --> 00:32:47.590
that that's what's important.

00:32:47.590 --> 00:32:49.561
The connections with family
and friends, your health,

00:32:49.561 --> 00:32:50.880
that's what's important.

00:32:50.880 --> 00:32:52.440
There's no reason to stress yourself out

00:32:52.440 --> 00:32:53.870
and give yourself a heart attack

00:32:53.870 --> 00:32:55.130
over getting a corner office

00:32:55.130 --> 00:32:56.920
or getting into a certain school,

00:32:56.920 --> 00:33:00.980
when that thing matters way
less than your heart health.

00:33:00.980 --> 00:33:02.620
So if I think there's a silver lining

00:33:02.620 --> 00:33:06.050
that comes out of this,
it re-grounds all of us

00:33:06.050 --> 00:33:08.250
on what really matters in life.

00:33:08.250 --> 00:33:10.623
Staying connected and learning.

00:33:10.623 --> 00:33:14.160
And appreciating what it means to be alive

00:33:14.160 --> 00:33:15.460
and be a human being.

00:33:15.460 --> 00:33:17.170
So with that, I'll let everyone go.

00:33:17.170 --> 00:33:18.240
I'll see you all tomorrow.

00:33:18.240 --> 00:33:20.539
Tomorrow we will have a great guest

00:33:20.539 --> 00:33:23.910
from The College Board
to talk about the SAT.

00:33:23.910 --> 00:33:25.570
And I look forward to seeing you tomorrow

00:33:25.570 --> 00:33:26.583
and future days.

